      "text": "To solve for the mean of the set \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\) given that the median is 9, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the sorted set:**\n   The set is \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\). The median of a set with 5 elements is the third element when the set is ordered. Therefore, the third element in the sorted set is \\(n + 6\\).\n\n2. **Set up the equation for the median:**\n   We are given that the median is 9. So, we can write:\n   \\[\n   n + 6 = 9\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\(n\\):**\n   \\[\n   n + 6 = 9 \\implies n = 3\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute \\(n\\) back into the set:**\n   Now that we have \\(n = 3\\), we substitute this value into the set:\n   \\[\n   \\{3, 3 + 5, 3 + 6, 3 + 9, 3 + 15\\} = \\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the mean:**\n   The mean of a set of numbers is the sum of the numbers divided by the number of elements. The sum of the set \\(\\{3, 8, 9, 12, 18\\}\\) is:\n   \\[\n   3 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 18 = 50\n   \\]\n   There are 5 elements in the set, so the mean is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Mean} = \\frac{50}{5} = 10\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the mean of the set is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
